WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:05.880
 So I hope you got from that, that it wasn't trivial. It wasn't like somebody just walked out and said, "Hey, make a network."

00:00:05.880 --> 00:00:07.880
 I mean, there were

00:00:07.880 --> 00:00:13.000
 forces, powerful forces, the telecom lobbyists that did not want this to happen.

00:00:13.000 --> 00:00:19.960
 And we will see these telecom lobbyists a couple times as we progress through this lecture.

00:00:19.960 --> 00:00:22.000
 So

00:00:22.000 --> 00:00:27.520
 Larry Smarr and the folks that made the supercomputers convinced Congress to authorize the

00:00:29.640 --> 00:00:33.160
 giving of a grant to build the National Science Foundations Network.

00:00:33.160 --> 00:00:39.840
 It was going to use the TCP/IP protocol that the ARPANET had built. It was supposed to be inclusive, at least for research

00:00:39.840 --> 00:00:45.440
 academics, and it was, you know, research universities, we want to get them connected.

00:00:45.440 --> 00:00:53.080
 And so our story now leads from the University of Illinois at Urbana-Champaign to my university, the University of Michigan.

00:00:53.080 --> 00:00:55.720
 Now if you take a look at this

00:00:55.720 --> 00:00:58.920
 ARPANET picture that was sort of right before

00:00:59.720 --> 00:01:06.000
 now, it's 1972, but if you look at all of the ARPANET pictures, there is a glaring omission

00:01:06.000 --> 00:01:08.960
 from my perspective.

00:01:08.960 --> 00:01:12.200
 Glaring omission on all of these. I wonder why aren't we there?

00:01:12.200 --> 00:01:17.840
 Well, there's a very good reason why the University of Michigan is not on any of the ARPANET pictures.

00:01:17.840 --> 00:01:20.120
 And that's because we build our own network.

00:01:20.120 --> 00:01:24.580
 We build a network using leased lines, a three-node network between

00:01:24.580 --> 00:01:28.600
 Michigan State University, University of Michigan, and Wayne State University.

00:01:28.880 --> 00:01:34.200
 We were sharing each other's compute resources. We were using each other's computers interactively.

00:01:34.200 --> 00:01:38.360
 We were playing online multi-user games. We called them adventure.

00:01:38.360 --> 00:01:43.760
 They were all based on text, but they were great fun, and they were great community building. We had chat forums,

00:01:43.760 --> 00:01:50.200
 we had all kinds of things, and we used each other's software, and it was a cool little world. And the ARPANET

00:01:50.200 --> 00:01:55.040
 wasn't seen by many as all that significant. It was kind of an experiment, right?

00:01:55.040 --> 00:01:57.760
 It was a research project, and we were a research, we were a production.

00:01:58.400 --> 00:02:01.840
 Merit was production, and ARPANET was research. So there you go, right?

00:02:01.840 --> 00:02:06.560
 So there was actually a lot of these sort of like nascent networks out there early days.

00:02:06.560 --> 00:02:14.860
 And so the University of Michigan sort of never really was significantly involved in the ARPANET work, but

00:02:14.860 --> 00:02:22.160
 the University of Michigan being a large university did want to get a supercomputer center. And because of some strategic

00:02:22.160 --> 00:02:25.540
 blunders, as Doug Van Holling will soon explain to you,

00:02:27.040 --> 00:02:28.840
 Michigan didn't get a supercomputer center.

00:02:28.840 --> 00:02:35.240
 And so the idea was is we would do what it was going to take to get the network, because that might be more fun.

00:02:35.240 --> 00:02:42.880
 So Doug Van Holling at the time was the chief information officer at the University of Michigan, having recently arrived from Carnegie Mellon University,

00:02:42.880 --> 00:02:45.680
 and he was also the

00:02:45.680 --> 00:02:50.080
 chief of the Merit network, the network that connected our three schools.

00:02:50.080 --> 00:02:53.920
 And so they figured that once they didn't get a supercomputer

00:02:54.640 --> 00:02:59.680
 center, they better really pull out the stops so that they would get the NSFnet.

00:02:59.680 --> 00:03:07.200
 And of course they did get the NSFnet, and Doug Van Holling was the principal investigator for the NSFnet starting in 1988.

00:03:07.200 --> 00:03:12.960
 So let's let Doug Van Holling tell you the story, and I'll be back in a moment.

